--- CRITERION 4: prose tests assert facts, not sentences --- MET
consequences_test.go asserts independent substrings: account id (:36), fmt.Sprintf
"%.2f" amount (:39-40), date (:43), pair key (:76, :87), verdict words CONFIRMED /
REJECTED plus the absence of the opposite (:79-84, :90-95). No fixed-sentence compare
anywhere in the file.
Copyedit survivability, tested rather than asserted: in scratch I rewrote the leading
sentence of BOTH consequence functions -- anchorConsequences' first two format lines
("a BalanceAnchor would be recorded on account %s stating its balance was %.2f as of
the END of %s" -> "COPYEDIT: this will record a balance anchor for the account %s,
asserting the balance to be %.2f at the close of business on %s.", plus a reworded
bank-convention clause) and both resolveConsequences branches ("transfer pair %s would
be CONFIRMED as a real transfer: both legs become Transfer/paired on the" -> "COPYEDIT:
the transfer pair %s is about to be CONFIRMED, i.e. treated as a genuine transfer; both
legs get paired on the", and the mirror for REJECTED). Result: all 8 tests PASS. A
substantial copyedit does not break them.
Judgment: they would survive an innocuous copyedit and catch semantic drift (criterion 5
below demonstrates the catch). Two residual couplings, both to the fact itself rather
than to wording, noted for the record: the verdict assertions require the words
CONFIRMED/REJECTED to stay upper-case, and the amount assertion requires plain %.2f
rendering (a change to "$4,210.55" would fail it). Neither is a fixed sentence.

