NARROWNESS (reproduced, reported for the lead's adjudication -- not scored as a
criterion failure): TestResolveTransferApprovedInBrowserRecordsTheDescribedVerdict
asserts out.Verdict (browser_approval_test.go:220), and applyResolveTransfer builds that
field as `Verdict: string(v)` (resolve.go:234) -- an echo of the tool's own input, not a
read-back of transfer_decisions.json. I mutated scratch resolve.go:227 to call
`deps.Transfers.ResolveTransfer(key, written)` with the OPPOSITE verdict while leaving
the output field alone: all 8 tests still PASS. So this test cannot distinguish a write
that records the opposite verdict from a correct one. What it DOES prove: the browser
round trip (it fails when awaitApproval is neutered), that a write happened at all
(Confirmed=true is reachable only after ResolveTransfer returns nil, resolve.go:227-236),
and that a .bak was taken (:226-228). Context: the pre-existing non-browser write test
TestResolveTransferSecondCallWithTokenWrites (resolve_test.go:75-103) has the identical
assertion depth and also never reads the decisions file, so this is the package's
established shape, which the brief told the worker to reuse -- not a regression R10
introduced. The lead's criterion 3 asked for the round trip "likewise" plus a
content-compare on the REFUSAL path; both are present and proven. If the lead wants the
approved path to read the verdict back off disk, that is a follow-up, and I flag it
rather than fail on it.
Second, smaller note: the two Refused round-trip tests still PASS under the approval.go:58
mutation, because a refusal reads the same ("refused", nothing written) whether it came
from awaitApproval or from confirm.DecisionFrom. They correctly pin the OUTCOME (no
write) but do not by themselves pin the browser rung; the two Approved tests do.

